1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,960
Before we talk about syntax, let's start
with a situation. Imagine you are building
software in the early nineteen nineties.
The internet is about to explode.

2
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:20,960
Your team wants one application to run on
Windows machines, Unix servers, and a
growing set of devices. You already have
C++. It is powerful. It can be fast.

3
00:00:21,079 --> 00:00:25,199
So a natural question appears: why invent
another language at all?

4
00:00:25,379 --> 00:00:39,619
The answer is not "because new languages
are fashionable." The answer is that C++
made some kinds of large-scale software
expensive in ways teams felt every week.
In C++, you manage memory yourself.

5
00:00:39,739 --> 00:00:51,139
That power is real — and so is the cost.
One small mistake can become a leak, a
crash, or a security hole that only
appears under load. Platform dependency is
another tax.

6
00:00:51,259 --> 00:01:01,699
Code that works on one operating system
can break on another. If your product must
run in many places, you may end up
rewriting and retesting the same business
logic again and again.

7
00:01:01,819 --> 00:01:12,259
Those two costs — memory risk and platform
rewrite — stacked on top of each other. A
team could survive one of them for a
while. Both at once slowed shipping to a
crawl.

8
00:01:12,379 --> 00:01:24,939
So the industry was facing a very
practical question: can we keep strong
application development, but reduce the
pain of memory mistakes and platform-
specific rewrites? That question is why
Java exists.

9
00:01:25,060 --> 00:01:37,700
Java was created to be safer for many
application teams than raw C++ work,
easier to maintain across large codebases,
and portable across platforms without
starting over for every operating system.

10
00:01:37,820 --> 00:01:50,420
First it was called Oak. Later it became
Java. The mission stayed practical: solve
an engineering headache, not invent a
slogan. And yet Java did arrive with a
slogan — Write Once, Run Anywhere.

11
00:01:50,540 --> 00:02:03,260
That line can sound like marketing until
you have paid the cost of shipping the
same product three times. For banks,
telecom systems, and enterprise backends,
rewrite cost is months, money, and risk.

12
00:02:03,380 --> 00:02:14,659
So the slogan mattered as a business
promise. But a promise is not a mechanism.
So the next natural question is: what does
Java actually do differently so the same
program can travel across machines?

13
00:02:14,840 --> 00:02:23,439
Here is the idea in simple language. Java
does not take your source and immediately
turn it into Windows-only machine code or
Mac-only machine code.

14
00:02:23,560 --> 00:02:34,800
First, the compiler turns your .java file
into bytecode, stored in a .class file.
Bytecode is a platform-neutral instruction
format — an international language for
programs.

15
00:02:34,920 --> 00:02:47,880
Then the JVM, the Java Virtual Machine,
reads that bytecode and translates it for
the local machine. Look at this bash for a
moment. Watch what each command is doing.
javac is the compiler.

16
00:02:48,000 --> 00:03:06,040
It checks your source and produces
bytecode. java does not mean "Windows ran
the class file as a native executable." It
means "start a JVM process, load this
class, and execute it." Windows has a JVM.
macOS has a JVM.

17
00:03:06,160 --> 00:03:22,400
Linux has a JVM. Containers have JVMs.
Same .class files. Different translators.
Same program behavior — as long as the JVM
is compatible. That pipeline is the real
meaning of Write Once, Run Anywhere.

18
00:03:22,520 --> 00:03:32,160
Once you see that, a quieter question
appears: is this only about portability,
or does the runtime change how we write
programs day to day? It changes both.

19
00:03:32,280 --> 00:03:37,600
Because the JVM sits between your program
and the machine, Java can also manage
memory for you.

20
00:03:37,720 --> 00:03:45,240
You still care about allocation and leaks
caused by lingering references — but you
are not calling free() by hand after every
object.

21
00:03:45,360 --> 00:03:55,000
That trade-off is a big reason large teams
could move faster without constantly
stepping on memory landmines. Let's make
the whole story concrete with the smallest
useful program.

22
00:03:55,120 --> 00:04:11,680
Suppose we only want the computer to print
a greeting. Even that tiny goal still
shows Java's structure: a class, an entry
point, and a library call. Look at this
java for a moment. Save this as
HelloWorld.java.

23
00:04:11,800 --> 00:04:17,639
The filename matters. A public top-level
class name and the filename must match,
including case.

24
00:04:17,759 --> 00:04:29,319
If the class is HelloWorld and the file is
helloworld.java, the compiler will fight
you — and that fight is teaching you
something about how Java finds and loads
types. Now walk the code with me.

25
00:04:29,439 --> 00:04:40,519
public class HelloWorld declares a named
blueprint. Almost everything in Java lives
inside a class. Inside it, public static
void main(String[] args) is the entry
point.

26
00:04:40,639 --> 00:04:52,719
When you launch the program, the JVM looks
for this kind of method. public means it
can be seen from outside. static means the
JVM can call it without first creating a
HelloWorld object.

27
00:04:52,839 --> 00:05:08,000
void means it does not return a value.
String[] args is how command-line
arguments arrive. Then
System.out.println("Hello, World!");
prints a line to the console. Compile it.
Run it. The screen shows Hello, World!.

28
00:05:08,119 --> 00:05:23,039
Behind that simple result is the full
thesis of this episode: source becomes
bytecode, the JVM loads it, finds main,
and executes the print. The operating
system is not reading your .java file as
if it were a native app.

29
00:05:23,159 --> 00:05:33,839
The JVM is doing the heavy lifting. This
is also where a common misunderstanding
begins. People say, "Java runs on
Windows." That sentence is incomplete.

30
00:05:33,959 --> 00:05:38,919
What usually runs on Windows is a JVM for
Windows, executing portable bytecode.

31
00:05:39,039 --> 00:05:52,919
Another mix-up arrives the moment someone
says "install Java." They may mean the
JDK, the JRE idea, or the JVM engine —
three related layers with different jobs.
And that mix-up is not a side note.

32
00:05:53,039 --> 00:06:03,479
It is the next problem waiting for us.
Today we answered why Java exists, how
portability actually works, and what
happens when a tiny program runs.

33
00:06:03,599 --> 00:06:14,439
The natural next question is still waiting
in plain language: when people say
"install Java," what are they actually
installing — and why do three different
acronyms show up as if they were the same
thing?

34
00:06:14,619 --> 00:06:18,299
Once those layers are clear, the confusion
starts to disappear.
